<blockquote>
<p>The ending could've been done a lot better considering that it straight up tells the reader what the sounds represent.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I didn't see why there'd be a need to mystify the sound, considering that the primary focus of the article isn't on a deep mystery.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Showing that could've drawn me into the story just enough to where everything else clicked.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not everything needs to be &quot;shown&quot; in a story if it isn't crucial. &quot;Show don't tell&quot; was also a <a href="https://www.chronicle.com/article/How-Iowa-Flattened-Literature/144531">genuine CIA psyop</a>, but that's besides the point.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I'm also not a fan of the last line, since it puts a moral to this whole thing.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Lung damage from construction is. A common thing? And not abnormal enough to be hamfisted in this context. If that feels like &quot;moralizing&quot; to you, and that's something you have issue with in the first place (stories have <em>always</em> been used to communicate messages) then that's not something I'm interested in changing.</p>
<p>Fixed the SPAG error.</p>
<p>(link on the psyop changed after finding a better article on the subject)</p>

