				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/redredred" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5379342); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5379342&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606834419" alt="redredred" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5379342)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/redredred" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5379342); return false;" >redredred</a></span> <span class="odate time_1593875770 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">04 Jul 2020 15:16</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4709463">
									

<blockquote>
<p>I didn't see why there'd be a need to mystify the sound, considering that the primary focus of the article isn't on a deep mystery.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Nearly 1/5th of the story is devoted to this mystery, and it is the main driving force of the story. It’s one of the primary questions of the article along with “What even is this thing?” And it’s clearly important enough to warrant a place in the ending paragraph. Therefore, one could imagine that it deserves something better than one line explaining exactly what was going on.</p>
<p>It’d be like if an adventure novel had a subplot wherein two characters are in a relationship. If in the ending there was a line which stated that “Character X and Y married and lived happily ever after”, I would also be infuriated. Yes, it’s not the main focus of the story, but it holds enough weight to warrant a better explanation.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Not everything needs to be &quot;shown&quot; in a story if it isn't crucial. &quot;Show don't tell&quot; was also a genuine CIA psyop, but that's besides the point.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Right, I agree that unimportant things should be struck. But this mystery is a crucial element of the story. If it wasn’t, then there wouldn’t be any inclusions of it at all in the story, let alone a place in the ending.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I also agree with you that the CIA using “show don’t tell” to silence writers is not relevant to this conversation nor this story.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Lung damage from construction is. A common thing? And not abnormal enough to be hamfisted in this context.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The fact that it’s being included at all means that it’s important to the story. Like you said, something shouldn’t be shown in a story if it isn’t crucial. And since it’s the ending line and placed immediately after a sentence which explains what the mysterious sounds were, it appears to be an explanation for what this anomaly is: Walter’s payback to the construction site for causing him to develop silicosis.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If that feels like &quot;moralizing&quot; to you, and that's something you have issue with in the first place (stories have <em>always</em> been used to communicate messages) then that's not something I'm interested in changing.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You miss the point by miles. And the next sentence too, which explains what I meant: “It's fine as is, but I would've preferred it simply being an incredibly odd event with no rhyme or reason for occurring when it did.”</p>
<p>I’m not saying that it’s bad for stories to have morals or an obvious message to the readers. I’m saying that this article doesn’t need one. There are plenty of articles (<a href="/scp-4710">SCP-4710</a> and <a href="/scp-3707">SCP-3707</a> come to mind) which just describe weird events with no message, no moral, and no explanation. It leaves the reader with an unanswered question, which makes the article stick in their mind.</p>
<p>I believe that not having a moral would give this article that quality, and would make everything else that much more weird.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
