			<div class="content" id="post-content-4709656">
									

<p>I'll admit that yes, I did miss your statement about preferring it as an odd, inexplicable event (your other critique caught my attention enough that I missed the rest), so allow me to clarify:</p>
<p>I don't agree with your feedback because it applies to a direction for the article I don't intend to take.</p>
<p>My goal was to write an event that, while on surface level, seems to make correlative sense, but, underneath, is still as odd as it was before. If I wrote it to lack any rhyme or reason I would've been unsatisfied with the final product. And, regarding your interpretation of my reply, you missed the point that the mystery over the sound is meant to be so minor that maintaining it would detract from the core of the article, and that it'd be pointless with regards to my intent.</p>
<p>The comment about lung damage was not in response to the part on importance; it was to the part on moralizing (and the implication that &quot;moralizing&quot; holds as a term in implying a hamfisted message). The lung damage is important to the article; the sounds, on the other hand, serve to maintain an intrigue to keep the article moving and allow for a sense of vague understanding before the final line.</p>
<p>Also, if you think 3707 is inexplicable with nothing else, then I'd highly recommend reading it over again and paying attention to the themes it tries to evoke.</p>

